---
import {Code} from "astro:components";
import type {SubscriptionState} from "streamotter/contracts";
import ReferencePage from "../layouts/ReferencePage.astro";
import {ERROR_FACTS,SUBSCRIPTION_STATE_FACTS,MAP_HANDLER_FAILURE} from "../release-facts.ts";
import handlerSource from "../snippets/recovery.ts?raw";
import {excerpt} from "../snippets/excerpt.ts";
import {RELEASE} from "../site.ts";
// The typical path, then the two ends a subscription can reach from it. Every state is shown, so a new one fails the build.
const PATH=["idle","authorizing","synchronizing","live","stale","resync-required"] as const satisfies readonly SubscriptionState[];
const ENDS=["failed","closed"] as const satisfies readonly SubscriptionState[];
const missing=Object.keys(SUBSCRIPTION_STATE_FACTS).filter(state=>!([...PATH,...ENDS] as string[]).includes(state));
if(missing.length) throw new Error(`when-it-breaks.astro does not place ${missing.join(", ")} on the state path.`);
const errorCount=Object.keys(ERROR_FACTS).length;
---
<ReferencePage title="When it breaks" description="Follow the state first, then the error. Some failures affect one view; others pause the shared source.">
<section aria-labelledby="states-title"><h2 id="states-title">Explore a view’s state</h2><p>This is an explanatory diagram, not a connection to the demo. A subscription moves along the typical path below; <code>live</code> and <code>stale</code> alternate as its source pauses and recovers.</p>
<nav class="state-row" aria-label="States on this page">{PATH.map((state,i)=><>{i>0&&<span aria-hidden="true">{PATH[i-1]==="live"?" ⇄ ":" → "}</span>}<a class="chip" data-state={state} href={`#state-${state}`}>{state}</a></>)}<span class="state-row-ends">Ends: {ENDS.map((state,i)=><>{i>0&&" "}<a class="chip" data-state={state} href={`#state-${state}`}>{state}</a></>)}</span></nav>
<ol class="state-path" role="list" aria-label="Typical path">{PATH.map(state=><li id={`state-${state}`}><span class="chip" data-state={state}>{state}</span><p>{SUBSCRIPTION_STATE_FACTS[state].description}</p></li>)}</ol>
<p class="state-ends-title">Two ends: <span class="chip" data-state="closed">closed</span>, from any state, when you unsubscribe or the client closes; <span class="chip" data-state="failed">failed</span>, when an attempt is denied or a handler fails for this subscription.</p>
<ul class="state-path state-ends" role="list" aria-label="Where a subscription ends">{ENDS.map(state=><li id={`state-${state}`}><span class="chip" data-state={state}>{state}</span><p>{SUBSCRIPTION_STATE_FACTS[state].description}</p></li>)}</ul>
<div class="reference-card worked"><h3>Worked example: Foul sensor on a Lab bench</h3><p>On a bench, your view of the creek goes <code>authorizing</code>, <code>synchronizing</code>, then <code>live</code>. Foul sensor removes LC-03's calibration table, so the next LC-03 reading fails in the map handler: the source pauses at that record, and your view turns <code>stale</code> with <code>SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE</code>, still showing its last revision. Restore calibration and resume the source, and the gateway retries the same record; your view takes a fresh snapshot and is <code>live</code> again.</p><p><a href="/lab/#control-fouled-sensor">Try it on a bench</a></p></div></section>
<section><h2>One failure, two perspectives</h2><p>{MAP_HANDLER_FAILURE.summary}</p><div class="reference-grid"><div class="reference-card"><h3>Already-open browser view</h3><p><code>stale / SOURCE_UNAVAILABLE</code></p><p>The view keeps its last data, visibly stale. A source pause is a state event; it does not invoke the subscription’s error listener.</p></div><div class="reference-card"><h3>Development gateway trace</h3><p><code>map / failed / HANDLER_FAILED</code></p><p>The trace identifies the operator’s cause without exposing payloads. The production gateway has no trace endpoint.</p></div></div><p>{MAP_HANDLER_FAILURE.recovery}</p><p>A subscription that starts synchronizing while the source is already paused can receive the source’s specific reason. The normalization above describes an already-open view becoming stale.</p><p>Pausing is the default response to a bad record. In {RELEASE} you can opt into more for two failure classes: see <a href="#source-failure-policies">the source-failure policies</a> below.</p><p><a href="/lab/#control-fouled-sensor">See it: Foul sensor</a></p></section>
<section><h2>Handle state and errors separately</h2><Code code={excerpt(handlerSource)} lang="ts" theme="night-owl" /><p>The sample is type-checked against the pinned package. A rejected wait does not necessarily stop the subscription underneath it.</p></section>
<section id="source-failure-policies" aria-labelledby="policies-title"><h2 id="policies-title">Source-failure policies</h2>
<p>Pausing is the default: a bad record pauses its source and views go stale. {RELEASE} adds opt-in policies per source and failure class (bounded retry, quarantine with a recovery guard, and no force-skip). The live creek runs none of them; the Failure Lab's benches can. <a href="/failure-reference/#source-failure-policies">Read the policies</a>.</p>
<p id="record-disposition">Record disposition, step by step, is in the <a href="/failure-reference/#record-disposition">failure reference</a>.</p></section>
<section id="what-this-demo-runs" aria-labelledby="demo-runs-title"><h2 id="demo-runs-title">What this demo runs</h2>
<p>The live creek's gateway and the walkthrough configure no failure policies: a bad record on the creek pauses its source, as described above. Only the Failure Lab's benches can run with failure handling, each bench's gateway on its own copy of the creek, and only when the Lab's deployment turns it on with <code>LAB_FAILURE_HANDLING</code>; it is off unless set. Set to <code>retry</code>, the benches get bounded retries for transient mapper errors. Set to <code>quarantine</code>, for a deployment whose Kafka authorization is verified for it, they also quarantine with a recovery guard. The Lab's <a href="/lab/#source-failures">Source failures track</a> lists the exercises built on these policies; it asks the demo's backend which of them its benches can run, and gives the reason for any it can't.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="errors-title"><h2 id="errors-title">Error reference</h2><p>All {errorCount} error codes in {RELEASE}, each with its message, its default retry behavior, and where it shows up, are in the <a href="/failure-reference/#error-reference">failure reference</a>.</p></section>
</ReferencePage>
<style>
.state-row { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:center; gap:6px 2px; margin-top:16px; color:var(--ink-2); }
/* The chips here are links: a touch target of at least 24px (WCAG 2.5.8); the badge look stays everywhere else. */
.state-row a.chip { text-decoration:none; padding:8px 10px; font-size:.78rem; }
.state-row-ends { margin-left:18px; display:inline-flex; gap:6px; align-items:center; }
.state-path { display:grid; gap:10px; padding:0; margin:16px 0 0; list-style:none; }
.state-path li { display:grid; grid-template-columns:10.5rem minmax(0,1fr); gap:6px 18px; align-items:start; padding:14px 18px; border:1px solid var(--line); border-radius:var(--radius); background:var(--surface); }
.state-path .chip { justify-self:start; margin-top:3px; }
.state-path p { margin:0; }
.state-ends-title { margin-top:20px; }
.state-ends { margin-top:10px; }
.worked { margin-top:24px; }
.worked p + p { margin-top:12px; }
@media (max-width:560px) { .state-path li { grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr); } }
</style>
